<LegalLayout
  title="Trademark Policy"
  description="How to refer to Merkleye, and what a fork has to change."
  updated="[EFFECTIVE DATE]"
>
  <p>
    &ldquo;Merkleye&rdquo; and the Merkleye eye mark are trademarks of
    <span class="tbd">[ENTITY LEGAL NAME]</span>
    <span class="tbd">[registration pending]</span>. The software is licensed
    under AGPL-3.0. <strong>That licence grants rights in the code and no
    rights in the name.</strong> The two are separate on purpose: the mark is
    how people know a build came from us, which matters more than usual for a
    security tool.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Uses that need no permission</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Saying truthfully that you use, deploy, support, or integrate with
      Merkleye.
    </li>
    <li>
      Referring to Merkleye in articles, talks, comparisons, documentation, and
      training material — including critical ones.
    </li>
    <li>
      Naming the software in a package that installs it unmodified, in the
      conventional distribution form (<code>merkleye</code>,
      <code>merkleye-server</code>).
    </li>
    <li>Linking to merkleye.com and using the logo to do it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Modified versions must be renamed</h2>
  <p>
    The AGPL lets you fork and redistribute. If you distribute a modified
    build, it must not be called Merkleye and must not use the mark or logo as
    its identity. You may — and should — state accurately that it is
    &ldquo;based on Merkleye&rdquo; or &ldquo;a fork of Merkleye&rdquo;.
  </p>
  <p>
    A modified build that keeps our name would send our users a signal we
    cannot stand behind: that the binary they are running is the one we sign
    and support. That is the entire reason this section exists.
  </p>
  <p>
    Distribution packaging that applies only patches necessary to build on a
    platform is not a &ldquo;modified version&rdquo; for this purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Not permitted</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Using the mark in your company name, product name, domain name, or app
      store listing.
    </li>
    <li>
      Registering the mark, a confusingly similar mark, or a domain
      incorporating it, in any jurisdiction. Given what this product does, we
      will pursue this one.
    </li>
    <li>
      Implying endorsement, partnership, certification, or affiliation that
      does not exist.
    </li>
    <li>
      Altering the logo — recolouring, distorting, combining it with other
      marks, or using it as your own icon.
    </li>
    <li>
      Selling merchandise bearing the mark, or offering a commercial service
      whose name suggests it is the official one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Using the mark correctly</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Write it &ldquo;Merkleye&rdquo; in prose and <code>merkleye</code> in
      code, commands, and package names. Not MerklEye, not MERKLEYE, not
      Merkl-Eye.
    </li>
    <li>Use it as an adjective where it reads naturally: &ldquo;the Merkleye
      watchtower&rdquo;, not &ldquo;a Merkleye&rdquo;.
    </li>
    <li>
      Assets and clear-space rules will be published at
      <span class="tbd">[/brand]</span>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Asking</h2>
  <p>
    If your use is not covered here, ask —
    <a href="mailto:legal@merkleye.com">legal@merkleye.com</a>. We grant
    reasonable requests, and we would rather say yes than find out later.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
